1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,800
This is ECE 358, video 13. The topic is the IP datagram and fragmentation. It covers slides 22 to 27 of Chapter 4.

2
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:20,226
After this video, you can name every field of the IPv4 header, with its size and its use.

3
00:00:20,676 --> 00:00:24,879
You can explain the MTU, fragmentation and reassembly.

4
00:00:25,329 --> 00:00:31,729
You can fragment a datagram and give the length, I D, flags and offset of each fragment.

5
00:00:32,179 --> 00:00:45,341
And you can check if a proposed fragmentation is valid. These are question bank questions 22 to 28. Question 21, about output buffers, is solved in video 12.

6
00:00:45,791 --> 00:00:50,037
Slide 22 shows the network layer of a host or a router.

7
00:00:50,487 --> 00:00:55,820
The transport layer is above it. The link layer and the physical layer are below it.

8
00:00:56,270 --> 00:01:07,555
The network layer has three parts. The first part is path selection. Routing protocols, such as OSPF and BGP, run the routing algorithms.

9
00:01:08,005 --> 00:01:10,523
They build the forwarding table, R T.

10
00:01:10,973 --> 00:01:20,018
The second part is the IP protocol. IP defines the datagram format, the addressing, and the rules for packet handling.

11
00:01:20,468 --> 00:01:25,844
IP uses the forwarding table to send each datagram to an output link.

12
00:01:26,294 --> 00:01:34,678
The third part is the ICMP protocol. ICMP reports errors, for example network unreachable.

13
00:01:35,128 --> 00:01:40,931
ICMP also lets routers send signals, for example routing advice.

14
00:01:41,381 --> 00:01:44,602
This video is about the IP datagram format.

15
00:01:45,052 --> 00:01:57,383
Slide 23 shows the IPv4 datagram format. Each row is 32 bits. 32 divided by 8 is 4, so each row is 4 bytes.

16
00:01:57,833 --> 00:02:05,107
The first row has the version, the header length, the type of service, and the total length of the datagram in bytes.

17
00:02:05,557 --> 00:02:13,728
The second row is for fragmentation. It has a 16-bit identifier, 3 flag bits, and the fragment offset.

18
00:02:14,178 --> 00:02:19,554
The third row has the TTL, the upper layer protocol, and the header checksum.

19
00:02:20,004 --> 00:02:27,855
Rows 4 and 5 hold the 32-bit source IP address and the 32-bit destination IP address.

20
00:02:28,305 --> 00:02:34,619
Then come the options, if any. Examples are a timestamp, or a record of the route taken.

21
00:02:35,069 --> 00:02:43,069
After the header comes the payload. It has a variable length. Usually it is a TCP or a UDP segment.

22
00:02:43,519 --> 00:02:52,266
Without options, the header has 5 rows of 4 bytes. 5 times 4 is 20, so the minimum header is 20 bytes.

23
00:02:52,716 --> 00:02:57,175
The maximum length of a whole datagram is 64 K bytes.

24
00:02:57,625 --> 00:03:02,041
A typical datagram is 1500 bytes or less.

25
00:03:02,491 --> 00:03:17,808
Slide 24 explains the fields one by one. The version field has 4 bits. For IPv4, the value is 4. In binary that is 0 1 0 0: the bit with place value 4 is 1.

26
00:03:18,258 --> 00:03:24,210
The header length field also has 4 bits. Its unit is 4 bytes, not 1 byte.

27
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:34,345
So a 20-byte header is written as 20 divided by 4, which is 5. In binary that is 0 1 0 1: 4 plus 1.

28
00:03:34,795 --> 00:03:43,265
Here is the picture. 5 units of 4 bytes give 5 times 4, which is 20 bytes. This is the header without options.

29
00:03:43,715 --> 00:03:55,576
The largest 4-bit value is 1 1 1 1: 8 plus 4 plus 2 plus 1, which is 15. So the largest header is 15 times 4, which is 60 bytes.

30
00:03:56,026 --> 00:04:03,237
The fixed part is 20 bytes, so the options can use at most 60 minus 20, which is 40 bytes.

31
00:04:03,687 --> 00:04:11,772
The type of service field has 8 bits. The slides call it D S C P, differentiated services code point.

32
00:04:12,222 --> 00:04:14,761
It has a 6-bit D S C P part.

33
00:04:15,211 --> 00:04:24,149
And it has a 2-bit E C N part. E C N is explicit congestion notification. 6 plus 2 is 8 bits.

34
00:04:24,599 --> 00:04:31,255
D S C P tells the type of data carried. D S C P 46 means high priority.

35
00:04:31,705 --> 00:04:34,863
D S C P 0 means low priority.

36
00:04:35,313 --> 00:04:42,310
The length field has 16 bits. It gives the length of the whole datagram in bytes: header plus payload.

37
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:59,571
The largest 16-bit value is 2 to the power 16, minus 1. That is 65,535 bytes, the 64 K maximum. 64 K is 64 times 1024.

38
00:05:00,021 --> 00:05:04,159
To find the payload size, subtract the header length from the length.

39
00:05:04,609 --> 00:05:10,860
The second row is for fragmentation. A long IP packet can be cut into smaller packets.

40
00:05:11,310 --> 00:05:20,163
All those small packets carry the same 16-bit identifier, here x. So the destination knows that they belong together.

41
00:05:20,613 --> 00:05:24,304
There are 3 flag bits. The first bit is not used.

42
00:05:24,754 --> 00:05:27,314
The second bit is don't fragment, D F.

43
00:05:27,764 --> 00:05:31,092
The third bit is more fragments to follow, M F.

44
00:05:31,542 --> 00:05:39,265
M F equal to 1 means more fragments follow. M F equal to 0 means this is the last fragment.

45
00:05:39,715 --> 00:05:49,784
The fragment offset has 13 bits. Multiply it by 8. The result is the position of the first byte of this fragment in the original data.

46
00:05:50,234 --> 00:06:00,133
For example, an offset of 185 means byte 185 times 8, which is byte 1480.

47
00:06:00,583 --> 00:06:08,689
Slide 25 starts with TTL, time to live. TTL is the maximum number of remaining hops.

48
00:06:09,139 --> 00:06:13,641
Here, host A sends a datagram to B with TTL equal to 2.

49
00:06:14,091 --> 00:06:18,229
Router R1 subtracts 1. 2 minus 1 is 1.

50
00:06:18,679 --> 00:06:23,906
R1 forwards the datagram to R2 with TTL equal to 1.

51
00:06:24,356 --> 00:06:33,913
Router R2 subtracts 1 again. 1 minus 1 is 0. When the TTL is 0, the router discards the datagram.

52
00:06:34,363 --> 00:06:36,689
The datagram never gets to B.

53
00:06:37,139 --> 00:06:49,853
So the TTL limits the number of hops of a datagram. The field has 8 bits, so its largest value is 2 to the power 8, minus 1, which is 255.

54
00:06:50,303 --> 00:06:55,317
The upper layer field tells the destination which protocol gets the payload.

55
00:06:55,767 --> 00:06:57,964
The value 6 means TCP.

56
00:06:58,414 --> 00:07:00,974
The value 17 means UDP.

57
00:07:01,424 --> 00:07:06,693
So the network layer at the destination uses this field to deliver the payload.

58
00:07:07,143 --> 00:07:10,727
The header checksum detects bit errors in the packet header.

59
00:07:11,177 --> 00:07:15,999
IP ignores errors in the data. The transport layer checks the data.

60
00:07:16,449 --> 00:07:23,297
The source IP address is the 32-bit address of the node that originally created the packet.

61
00:07:23,747 --> 00:07:29,485
The destination IP address is the 32-bit address of the destination node.

62
00:07:29,935 --> 00:07:38,319
Slide 23 also shows the overhead. A TCP segment inside an IP datagram carries two headers.

63
00:07:38,769 --> 00:07:41,180
The TCP header is 20 bytes.

64
00:07:41,630 --> 00:07:43,742
The IP header is 20 bytes.

65
00:07:44,192 --> 00:07:50,891
So the overhead is 20 plus 20, which is 40 bytes, plus the application layer overhead.

66
00:07:51,341 --> 00:08:04,034
For example, a 1500-byte datagram can carry at most 1500 minus 40, which is 1460 bytes of application data.

67
00:08:04,484 --> 00:08:17,241
Slide 26. Every network link has an MTU, a maximum transfer unit. It is the largest possible link-level frame. Different link types have different MTUs.

68
00:08:17,691 --> 00:08:23,366
Here, the link from R1 to R2 has an MTU of 4000 bytes.

69
00:08:23,816 --> 00:08:30,003
The link from R2 to R3 has an MTU of 1500 bytes.

70
00:08:30,453 --> 00:08:34,421
A 4000-byte datagram travels from A towards B.

71
00:08:34,871 --> 00:08:47,436
4000 bytes is more than 1500, so the datagram does not fit on the 1500-byte link. So R2 divides it. This is fragmentation.

72
00:08:47,886 --> 00:08:52,345
One large datagram goes in. Three smaller datagrams come out.

73
00:08:52,795 --> 00:08:57,061
The fragments are reassembled only at the destination, host B.

74
00:08:57,511 --> 00:09:01,842
The routers on the path, such as R3, do not reassemble them.

75
00:09:02,292 --> 00:09:06,793
Each fragment is a full datagram with its own header, the purple part.

76
00:09:07,243 --> 00:09:15,670
The I D, the three flags, and the offset in the header let the destination identify and order the related fragments.

77
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:29,112
Why does the offset count in units of 8 bytes? The length field has 16 bits. So a datagram can have byte positions from 0 up to 65,535.

78
00:09:29,562 --> 00:09:38,757
But the offset field has only 13 bits. That gives 2 to the power 13, which is 8192 values.

79
00:09:39,207 --> 00:09:50,236
To reach every part of the datagram, each offset value counts one block of 8 bytes. Offset 1 is byte 8, offset 2 is byte 16, and so on.

80
00:09:50,686 --> 00:10:04,041
8192 blocks times 8 bytes is 65,536 bytes. That covers bytes 0 to 65,535.

81
00:10:04,491 --> 00:10:14,005
The result is the rule on slide 27. Offset times 8 is the first byte, so a fragment can only start at a multiple of 8.

82
00:10:14,455 --> 00:10:19,789
So every fragment except the last must carry a multiple of 8 data bytes.

83
00:10:20,239 --> 00:10:35,193
Slide 27 gives an example. A datagram has length 4000, I D x, flags 0 0 0 and offset 0. The MTU of the output link is 1500 bytes.

84
00:10:35,643 --> 00:10:47,953
The header is 20 bytes. So the data is 4000 minus 20, which is 3980 bytes: bytes 0 to 3979.

85
00:10:48,403 --> 00:11:06,493
Each fragment has its own 20-byte header. So a fragment can carry at most 1500 minus 20, which is 1480 data bytes. 1480 divided by 8 is 185, a whole number.

86
00:11:06,943 --> 00:11:18,890
Fragment 1 carries bytes 0 to 1479. Its length is 1480 plus 20, which is 1500.

87
00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:27,553
Its offset is 0 divided by 8, which is 0. More fragments follow, so the flags are 0 0 1.

88
00:11:28,003 --> 00:11:42,019
Fragment 2 carries bytes 1480 to 2959. Its length is again 1480 plus 20, which is 1500.

89
00:11:42,469 --> 00:11:52,432
Its offset is 1480 divided by 8, which is 185. The flags are 0 0 1 again.

90
00:11:52,882 --> 00:12:13,682
Fragment 3 carries the rest: 3980 minus 2960, which is 1020 bytes, bytes 2960 to 3979. Its length is 1020 plus 20, which is 1040.

91
00:12:14,132 --> 00:12:25,759
Its offset is 2960 divided by 8, which is 370. It is the last fragment, so the flags are 0 0 0.

92
00:12:26,209 --> 00:12:42,081
Check: 1480 plus 1480 plus 1020 is 3980. All fragments have the same I D. M F is 1 in every fragment except the last.

93
00:12:42,531 --> 00:12:52,792
Here is the method for every fragmentation question. Step 1. Find the data size D. It is the datagram length minus the 20-byte header.

94
00:12:53,242 --> 00:13:07,514
Step 2. Find the maximum data per fragment. It is the MTU minus 20. Then round it down to a multiple of 8. Each fragment except the last must carry a multiple of 8 bytes.

95
00:13:07,964 --> 00:13:16,092
Step 3. Cut the data into pieces of this maximum size. The last piece takes the rest. It can be any size.

96
00:13:16,542 --> 00:13:24,926
Step 4. For each fragment, the length is its data plus 20. The offset is its first data byte divided by 8.

97
00:13:25,376 --> 00:13:38,731
Step 5. All fragments keep the same I D. The flags are 0 0 1, more fragments, in every fragment except the last. The last fragment has flags 0 0 0.

98
00:13:39,181 --> 00:13:46,690
Now look at the destination. Host B keeps a buffer for each I D. The fragments can arrive in any order.

99
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:59,599
Here the last fragment arrives first. Its offset is 370, so its data starts at byte 370 times 8, which is 2960.

100
00:14:00,049 --> 00:14:13,702
M F is 0, so it is the last fragment. Now B knows the total data size: 2960 plus 1020, which is 3980 bytes.

101
00:14:14,152 --> 00:14:23,773
Next, the fragment with offset 0 arrives. 0 times 8 is 0, so its 1480 bytes go at the start.

102
00:14:24,223 --> 00:14:37,429
Then the fragment with offset 185 arrives. 185 times 8 is 1480, so its data goes at byte 1480.

103
00:14:37,879 --> 00:14:45,516
Now there are no gaps from byte 0 to byte 3979. The datagram is complete.

104
00:14:45,966 --> 00:14:51,939
IP gives the payload to the protocol in the upper layer field, for example TCP.

105
00:14:52,389 --> 00:15:05,189
Question bank, network layer, question 22. Briefly explain the functions of routing, namely OSPF, of IP, and of ICMP. Pause the video and try it.

106
00:15:05,639 --> 00:15:11,847
Use slide 22. OSPF is a routing protocol. It does path selection.

107
00:15:12,297 --> 00:15:16,351
It runs a routing algorithm and builds the forwarding table.

108
00:15:16,801 --> 00:15:23,051
IP defines the datagram format, the addressing, and the packet handling conventions.

109
00:15:23,501 --> 00:15:28,387
Each router uses the forwarding table to forward IP datagrams.

110
00:15:28,837 --> 00:15:33,637
ICMP reports errors, for example network unreachable.

111
00:15:34,087 --> 00:15:38,332
It also carries router signaling, for example routing advice.

112
00:15:38,782 --> 00:15:54,163
Question 23 asks for a table of the IPv4 header. For each field, give its size and its use. Then give the maximum payload with a minimal header and with a maximal header. Pause the video and try it.

113
00:15:54,613 --> 00:16:03,147
Read the sizes from the 32-bit rows. Version is 4 bits: the IP version, 4 for IPv4.

114
00:16:03,597 --> 00:16:14,413
Header length is 4 bits. It counts 4-byte units, so 5 means 5 times 4, which is 20 bytes. The maximum, 15, means 60 bytes.

115
00:16:14,863 --> 00:16:23,396
Type of service is 8 bits: a 6-bit D S C P that gives the type of data and the priority, and a 2-bit E C N.

116
00:16:23,846 --> 00:16:35,537
These three fields and the length fill row 1. So length is 32 minus 4 minus 4 minus 8, which is 16 bits. It is the datagram length in bytes.

117
00:16:35,987 --> 00:16:42,472
Row 2: the identifier is 16 bits, the same in all fragments of one datagram.

118
00:16:42,922 --> 00:16:47,786
The flags are 3 bits: not used, don't fragment, and more fragments.

119
00:16:48,236 --> 00:16:50,092
Now the rest of the table.

120
00:16:50,542 --> 00:17:02,254
The fragment offset fills the rest of row 2: 32 minus 16 minus 3, which is 13 bits. Offset times 8 is the first data byte of the fragment.

121
00:17:02,704 --> 00:17:13,371
TTL is 8 bits. It is the maximum number of remaining hops. Each router subtracts 1, and at 0 the router discards the packet.

122
00:17:13,821 --> 00:17:22,461
Upper layer is 8 bits. It names the protocol that gets the payload: 6 for TCP, 17 for UDP.

123
00:17:22,911 --> 00:17:28,201
The header checksum is 16 bits. It detects bit errors in the header only.

124
00:17:28,651 --> 00:17:35,265
The source IP address is 32 bits. It is the node that originally created the packet.

125
00:17:35,715 --> 00:17:41,283
The destination IP address is 32 bits. It is the destination node.

126
00:17:41,733 --> 00:17:55,322
Options are variable. The header can be up to 60 bytes, so options take 0 to 60 minus 20, which is 40 bytes. Two examples are a timestamp and a record of the route taken.

127
00:17:55,772 --> 00:18:14,588
The payload. The length field allows at most 65,535 bytes. With a minimal 20-byte header, the payload is at most 65,535 minus 20, which is 65,515 bytes.

128
00:18:15,038 --> 00:18:26,921
With a maximal 60-byte header, it is at most 65,535 minus 60, which is 65,475 bytes.

129
00:18:27,371 --> 00:18:43,115
Questions 24, 25 and 26. Explain the MTU. Say if the transport layer has a similar concept. And say why it is useful that IP can fragment a long packet. Pause the video and try it.

130
00:18:43,565 --> 00:18:53,314
Question 24. The MTU, maximum transfer unit, is the size limit of a link: the largest possible link-level frame.

131
00:18:53,764 --> 00:18:58,777
So it is the largest IP datagram that one frame on that link can carry.

132
00:18:59,227 --> 00:19:07,419
Different link types have different MTUs. A typical value is 1500 bytes, on an Ethernet link.

133
00:19:07,869 --> 00:19:22,632
Question 25. Yes. In the transport layer, TCP has the MSS, the maximum segment size. It is the largest amount of data in one segment. This is from the Chapter 3 slides.

134
00:19:23,082 --> 00:19:37,631
Question 26. A path can have links with different MTUs. With fragmentation, one large datagram can still cross a link with a smaller MTU. The router cuts it into datagrams that fit.

135
00:19:38,081 --> 00:19:45,655
The source does not need to know the smallest MTU on the path. The destination reassembles the fragments.

136
00:19:46,105 --> 00:20:22,259
Question 27. Router R1 receives a datagram with a 20-byte header, length 6020, I D 2700, flags 0 0 0 and offset 0. The MTU of the output links is 1550 bytes. Fragment it, and give the length, I D, flags, offset and payload of each fragment. The question says a maximal number of packets. We read it as: make each fragment as large as the MTU allows. Pause the video and try it.

137
00:20:22,709 --> 00:20:28,725
Step 1. The data is 6020 minus 20, which is 6000 bytes.

138
00:20:29,175 --> 00:20:38,540
Step 2. The maximum data per fragment is 1550 minus 20, which is 1530.

139
00:20:38,990 --> 00:21:00,942
But 1530 divided by 8 is 191 point 2 5. That is not a whole number. So round down to a multiple of 8: 191 times 8 is 1528 bytes. Do not use 1530. That is the trap in this question.

140
00:21:01,392 --> 00:21:24,176
Step 3. Cut 6000 bytes into pieces of 1528. Three full pieces use 3 times 1528, which is 4584 bytes. The last piece gets 6000 minus 4584, which is 1416 bytes.

141
00:21:24,626 --> 00:21:42,098
Step 4. The lengths are 1528 plus 20, which is 1548, three times. The last length is 1416 plus 20, which is 1436.

142
00:21:42,548 --> 00:21:46,793
The I D stays 2700 in all four fragments.

143
00:21:47,243 --> 00:22:10,027
The offsets are 0 divided by 8, which is 0. Then 1528 divided by 8, which is 191. Then 3056 divided by 8, which is 382. Then 4584 divided by 8, which is 573.

144
00:22:10,477 --> 00:22:16,984
The flags are 0 0 1 for the first three fragments, and 0 0 0 for the last.

145
00:22:17,434 --> 00:22:24,410
Check: every length is at most 1550, and the payloads add up to 6000.

146
00:22:24,860 --> 00:22:46,193
Question 28. A datagram has a 20-byte header and 3000 bytes of payload. The MTU is 1500. Can it be cut into three fragments with 1420, 1420 and 160 bytes of payload? Pause the video and try it.

147
00:22:46,643 --> 00:22:52,339
Check the offsets. The first fragment starts at byte 0, so its offset is 0.

148
00:22:52,789 --> 00:23:05,461
The second fragment starts at byte 1420. Its offset would be 1420 divided by 8, which is 177 point 5.

149
00:23:05,911 --> 00:23:11,757
The 13-bit offset field cannot hold this. It counts whole 8-byte units only.

150
00:23:12,207 --> 00:23:25,625
The MTU is not the problem. Each fragment is 1420 plus 20, which is 1440 bytes, and that is at most 1500.

151
00:23:26,075 --> 00:23:35,953
The sizes also add up: 1420 plus 1420 plus 160 is 3000.

152
00:23:36,403 --> 00:23:45,939
But the data in every fragment except the last must be a multiple of 8, and 1420 is not. So the answer is no.

153
00:23:46,389 --> 00:24:11,626
A valid choice with three fragments is 1480, 1480 and 3000 minus 2960, which is 40 bytes. The offsets are 0, 1480 divided by 8 which is 185, and 2960 divided by 8 which is 370.

154
00:24:12,076 --> 00:24:29,548
To recap. The IPv4 header is at least 20 bytes and at most 60 bytes. The header length field counts 4-byte units. The length field has 16 bits, so a datagram is at most 65,535 bytes.

155
00:24:29,998 --> 00:24:37,657
The identifier has 16 bits, the flags 3 bits, and the offset 13 bits, in units of 8 bytes.

156
00:24:38,107 --> 00:24:52,208
Each router subtracts 1 from the TTL, and discards the datagram at 0. The upper layer field is 6 for TCP and 17 for UDP. The checksum covers only the header.

157
00:24:52,658 --> 00:25:01,063
Each link has an MTU. Routers fragment a datagram that is too large, and only the destination reassembles it.

158
00:25:01,513 --> 00:25:16,105
To fragment: take the MTU minus 20, round down to a multiple of 8, and cut the data. Each offset is the first byte divided by 8. M F is 1 in every fragment except the last.

159
00:25:16,555 --> 00:25:20,758
The next video is about IPv4 addressing and subnets.
